# Alignment from this package's vocabulary into the SemviaIO/party hub.
#
# In its own file, and NOT imported by schema/ontology.ttl, deliberately. A
# consumer who wants the GLEIF virtual graph and nothing else installs this
# package and never loads this document; a consumer who wants their GLEIF reads
# to meet another package's reads installs the hub too. The alignment is
# opt-in because every lattice edge here is walked at read time (#4986) and
# therefore costs rewrite budget — a consumer who is not federating should not
# pay for the ability to.
#
# WHY A HUB EDGE AND NOT A DIRECT ONE. The alternative is aligning straight to
# whichever package we want to meet — and it works, for exactly two packages.
# The cost is coupling: a direct edge names the other package's IRIs, so this
# package cannot be read without a pin to that package's version, and a third
# package doubles the file count again. One edge into the hub is read by every
# other aligned package at once, and this file names no package but the hub.

@prefix rdf:   <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:   <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix sh:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dct:   <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@prefix svs:   <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/meta-schema#> .
@prefix svds:  <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/dataset#> .
@prefix gleif: <https://github.com/SemviaIO/gleif/ontology#> .
@prefix party: <https://github.com/SemviaIO/party/ontology#> .

<> a svs:Schema ;
  dct:title "GLEIF ⇄ Party Alignment"@en ;
  dct:description "Lattice edges relating this package's legal-entity vocabulary to the SemviaIO/party hub, so a GLEIF read and any other hub-aligned package's read answer over one graph."@en ;
  svds:imports <https://pkg.semvia.io/semvia/core/meta-schema> ;
  svds:imports <./ontology> ;
  sh:declare [
    a sh:PrefixDeclaration ;
    sh:prefix "party" ;
    sh:namespace party:
  ] ;
.

################################################################
# The hub terms this file names
################################################################
#
# Declared locally and MINIMALLY — a bare type, no label, no comment. The type
# is required because the meta-schema needs the ends of a subclass or
# sub-property edge to be a class and a property respectively. The annotations
# are deliberately absent: when the hub is installed it supplies the label and
# comment for its own terms, and a second set asserted from here would duplicate
# annotations on a term this package does not own.

party:Organization a rdfs:Class .
party:lei          a rdf:Property .
party:name         a rdf:Property .

################################################################
# The edges
################################################################

# 1. Every entity in the Global LEI System is a party:Organization.
#
# `rdfs:subClassOf`, NOT `owl:equivalentClass`, and the direction matters in
# both of the ways #4986 walks an equivalence.
#
# Forward the equivalence would be harmless. Backward it would assert that every
# organization anywhere is a registered LEI holder — false of most of the world,
# and catastrophically false of the shell companies a sanctions dataset is
# mostly about. A one-directional subclass edge buys the whole widening we want
# (rdfs9 lifts every GLEIF entity into party:Organization and party:Party, so a
# hub-level read spans this dataset) while asserting nothing about the converse.
#
# Note what this edge does NOT do: it does not commit `gleif:LegalEntity` to
# being a *company*. The class spans GENERAL, FUND, SOLE_PROPRIETOR, BRANCH,
# RESIDENT_GOVERNMENT_ENTITY and INTERNATIONAL_ORGANIZATION, because one relation
# yields one class for every row it carries and no subclass ladder is declared
# yet (#4). `party:Organization` is deliberately broad enough to hold all six, and
# stays the right hub term even after a ladder lands — the hub draws no
# company/government distinction of its own.
gleif:LegalEntity rdfs:subClassOf party:Organization .

# 2. The LEI is the same string under two names.
#
# Both are the ISO 17442 identifier carried as a literal on the entity itself,
# not on a reified identifier object. Symmetric and exact, so equivalence rather
# than subsumption.
#
# This is the edge that does the real work. Any other package that carries an
# LEI and aligns its own predicate to `party:lei` becomes joinable with this one
# through a single pattern, on a value both sources already publish — and
# neither package ever names the other. That is the whole thesis of the hub: an
# identifier issued by a standards body dissolves the bridge rather than
# relocating it.
gleif:lei owl:equivalentProperty party:lei .

# 3. A registered legal name is a name, but not conversely.
#
# `gleif:legalName` is specifically the name on the register. `party:name` is
# any name a party is known by. So the containment runs one way: rdfs7
# propagates GLEIF legal names up into `party:name`, and nothing asserts that
# some other source's trading name or transliteration is a registered legal
# name. A query that needs the legal name specifically still reads
# `gleif:legalName`.
gleif:legalName rdfs:subPropertyOf party:name .

################################################################
# Considered and rejected
################################################################
#
#   gleif:Relationship → any hub class
#       The hub declares no relationship vocabulary, on purpose. A GLEIF
#       relationship is consolidation, fund management or branching, reified
#       with its own status and type; other sources model ownership as an edge
#       with a percentage. Neither contains the other, and a hub term loose
#       enough to hold both would say nothing.
#
#   gleif:entityCategory → any hub class ladder
#       This is the discriminator a local subclass ladder would be built from
#       (#4). Aligning the PREDICATE to a hub term would not recover that ladder,
#       and declaring hub subclasses that nothing types an instance into is a
#       lattice the data never populates.
#
#   gleif:legalJurisdiction → a hub jurisdiction term
#       No such hub term exists, deliberately: sources disagree about whether a
#       jurisdiction is an ISO 3166 token, a subdivision code, or an IRI. Left
#       to the source vocabulary until a second package needs to meet on it.
